{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"J3 aac8 HELD-OUT VALIDATION -> GREEN after one test-side correction. 27 tests: 18 happy-path + 9 held out.\nTIERING: standard/mid (sonnet), named on dispatch. Implementer reported 1 ATTEMPT — happy path green on first run after writing all three modules. Zero escalations across J2/J3/J4.\nIt also self-caught something worth recording: the AC's ADF allowlist was tripped by explanatory DOCSTRING PROSE in jira_fields.py that still named adf.fit_text_to_adf_limit even though the code no longer called it. It reworded the rationale without the literal string rather than leaving a false positive. Good instinct — the AC is about references, and a grep-shaped AC cannot tell code from prose.\n\nMY OWN TEST DEFECT (second one this run, same class): test_exactly_one_real_map_fields_to_remote failed with TWO 'real' bodies — adapters/jira/backend.py and adapters/jira_family/outbound_mapper.py. I did NOT accept that at face value. Inspected backend.py's body: it is a single 'return self._mapper.map_fields_to_remote(...)' with zero mapping logic — precisely the delegation the ticket's own AC sanctions ('must either delegate to the shared implementation or not define the method at all'). So MY TEST was wrong: it conflated 'has a body' with 'duplicates logic', which would have counted the cure as the disease.\nFIX: added a _is_pure_delegation AST classifier and split the census three ways (protocol stub / pure delegation / real logic). The test now asserts exactly ONE body carrying logic, that it lives in jira_family/, that the neutral Protocol stub REMAINS, and — stronger than before — that adapters/jira/backend.py is specifically a delegation-or-stub. That last assertion is new teeth I would not have added without the failure.\nRED-FIRST INTEGRITY RE-PROVEN, not assumed: I moved outbound_mapper.py out of the tree and re-ran the CORRECTED test — still RED ('expected exactly ONE map_fields_to_remote carrying the mapping logic'). So the correction did not launder a green.\n\nMUTATION / TEETH (2 perturbations, both verified APPLIED, both reverted):\n 1. Collapsed normalization INTO fit_outbound — the exact trap the 4-op design exists to prevent => test_the_sanitizer_applies_the_fit_only_not_the_normalization RED with 'one two three' != 'one\\ntwo\\nthree'. That is the silent Cloud behavior change the original 2-op plan would have shipped, caught precisely.\n 2. Added 'from rebar_reconciler.adapters.jira.adf import adf_to_text' to jira_family/rich_text.py => 3 RED across TWO stories: J3's shared-layer assertion AND J2's import-graph + vendor-module tests. Cross-story structural enforcement is working — J2's landed contract actively polices J3's new code.\n Restored => 27/27 green.\nPRE-FLIGHT: make lint GREEN, make typecheck GREEN (389 files, up from 386), tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/ 1561 passed. Full suite running.\nSTACK: 1009 (J1 MERGED) <- 1010 (J2 green) <- 1011 (J4 pending gates) <- J3 commits next."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785352057650513001,"uuid":"5148bdbb-a01d-46ec-af3e-f2772acf98a6"}